Online Retail Customer Segmentation and Purchase Behaviour Analysis
A Python and PyCharm project for analysing the UCI Online Retail transaction dataset, assessing sales and customer behaviour, engineering customer-level features, and creating actionable customer segments using PCA and K-Means clustering.
Project purpose
The project addresses this business question:
> How can historical retail transactions be analysed to identify meaningful customer groups, understand purchasing patterns, and improve targeted marketing campaigns?
The workflow examines sales trends, products, countries, order values, purchasing frequency, and transaction quantities. It then groups customers into commercially useful segments that can support loyalty, cross-selling, second-purchase, and reactivation campaigns.
Main analytical objectives
Assess customer purchasing behaviour using recency, frequency, monetary value, basket value, and product variety.
Evaluate sales performance over time and identify leading products and geographic markets.
Build customer segments using unsupervised machine learning.
Translate the segments into practical marketing actions.
Dataset
The project uses the Online Retail dataset from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.
The raw Excel dataset is deliberately not included in this repository. Download `Online Retail.xlsx` from UCI and place it in:
```text
data/Online Retail.xlsx
```
Dataset source: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/352/online+retail
Key results from the original analysis
541,909 transaction lines
25,900 unique invoices
19,960 valid completed orders
4,338 identifiable customers used for segmentation
£10,642,110.80 completed-sales revenue
Four actionable customer segments
Silhouette score: 0.371
Davies-Bouldin Index: 0.916
Calinski-Harabasz score: 4,350.70
PCA variance retained: 86.02%
The most commercially important finding was that approximately 20.65% of customers generated 67.61% of identifiable-customer revenue.
Customer segments
Champions / Loyal High-Value — recent, frequent, high-spending customers with broad product diversity.
Regular / Promising — moderately active customers with potential for cross-selling and frequency growth.
Recent Occasional / Low-Value — recent buyers with limited purchasing history who may respond to second-purchase incentives.
At-Risk / Lapsed — customers with long periods since their last purchase and low recent contribution.
Analysis workflow
Load the large Excel workbook using a memory-efficient XLSX streaming reader.
Inspect missing values, exact duplicates, cancellations, quantities, and prices.
Remove exact duplicates and separate invoices beginning with `C` as cancellations.
Exclude non-positive quantities and prices from completed-sales analysis.
Retain missing-CustomerID rows for general sales analysis but exclude them from customer modelling.
Calculate revenue as `Quantity × UnitPrice`.
Create sales and purchasing-behaviour visualisations.
Engineer Recency, Frequency, Monetary Value, and Product Diversity features.
Cap extreme customer features at the 99th percentile and apply `log1p` transformation.
Standardise features, apply PCA, compare K-Means solutions, and evaluate the final segmentation model.
Export charts, CSV files, an Excel workbook, and the trained model bundle.
